String concatenation — `a + b`
==============================

`a + b` where both operands are `string` produces an owned new
buffer containing the concatenation. Operands aren't mutated;
each call to `+` allocates a fresh result. The length of the
result is the sum of the two operand lengths.

This file pins the basic-concat surface: length math, empty-
operand edge cases, ASCII vs multi-byte content, and order
matters (`a + b ≠ b + a` when the operands differ).

The intermediate-buffer-lifetime case (long `a + b + c + d`
chain) is in a separate file — `string_concat_chain.lsysl`.

    module sysl.tests.lang_features

Two non-empty ASCII strings
---------------------------

The simplest case: concatenate two non-empty ASCII strings.
The result is the byte-wise join.

    #test
    test_concat_two_words() -> unit
        val a = "hello"
        val b = "world"
        val c = a + b
        assert(c == "helloworld", s"got '$c'")
        assert(len(c) == 10, s"len should be 10, got ${len(c)}")

With a separator literal
------------------------

A common case: concat with a space (or other separator) in the
middle, expressed as `a + " " + b`. Two `+` operations, two
intermediate allocations, one final value.

    #test
    test_concat_with_space() -> unit
        val a = "hello"
        val b = "world"
        val c = a + " " + b
        assert(c == "hello world", s"got '$c'")
        assert(len(c) == 11, s"len should be 11, got ${len(c)}")

Empty-operand on each side
--------------------------

Concatenating with `""` is a no-op modulo allocation — the
result equals the other operand. Both the left-empty and
right-empty cases must be exact byte-equal to the non-empty
operand.

    #test
    test_concat_empty_left() -> unit
        val a = ""
        val b = "tail"
        val c = a + b
        assert(c == "tail", s"left-empty: got '$c'")
        assert(len(c) == 4, "left-empty length")

    #test
    test_concat_empty_right() -> unit
        val a = "head"
        val b = ""
        val c = a + b
        assert(c == "head", s"right-empty: got '$c'")
        assert(len(c) == 4, "right-empty length")

Both empty
----------

`"" + ""` is an empty string. Length 0, equal to `""`.

    #test
    test_concat_both_empty() -> unit
        val c = "" + ""
        assert(c == "", "both empty")
        assert(len(c) == 0, "both empty length")

Order matters
-------------

String `+` is not commutative — `a + b` and `b + a` produce
different buffers (unless operands happen to be equal). This
test pins that the codegen preserves operand order; a swap-
operands bug would flip both lhs/rhs into the result.

    #test
    test_concat_order_matters() -> unit
        val a = "foo"
        val b = "bar"
        assert(a + b == "foobar", "ab order")
        assert(b + a == "barfoo", "ba order")
        assert(a + b != b + a, "ab != ba")

Original operands not mutated
-----------------------------

Concatenation produces a new buffer; the source operands keep
their original contents. After `c = a + b`, `a` and `b` must
still equal their literal initialisers.

    #test
    test_concat_does_not_mutate_operands() -> unit
        val a = "alpha"
        val b = "beta"
        val c = a + b
        assert(a == "alpha", s"a should still be 'alpha', got '$a'")
        assert(b == "beta", s"b should still be 'beta', got '$b'")
        assert(c == "alphabeta", s"c should be 'alphabeta', got '$c'")

Length is the sum of operand lengths
------------------------------------

For ASCII content, `len(a + b) == len(a) + len(b)`. The
codegen must size the result buffer correctly — a one-off-by-
one would surface here.

    #test
    test_concat_length_is_sum() -> unit
        val a = "abc"
        val b = "defgh"
        val c = a + b
        assert(len(a) == 3, "len a")
        assert(len(b) == 5, "len b")
        assert(len(c) == 8, s"len c should be 8, got ${len(c)}")
        assert(c == "abcdefgh", s"got '$c'")

Multi-byte UTF-8 content
------------------------

Byte length is what `len` reports. A two-byte UTF-8 char
appended to a one-byte ASCII char produces a 3-byte string.
This pins that concat operates on bytes (no UTF-8-aware fast
path that might miscount).

    #test
    test_concat_utf8_bytes() -> unit
        val a = "a"
        val b = "ñ"      // U+00F1, encoded as 0xC3 0xB1 — 2 bytes
        val c = a + b
        assert(len(a) == 1, "ASCII 'a' is 1 byte")
        assert(len(b) == 2, "ñ is 2 bytes")
        assert(len(c) == 3, s"a + ñ is 3 bytes, got ${len(c)}")
        assert(c == "añ", "byte-equal to literal")

Self-concat
-----------

`a + a` where `a` is the same variable — the source must not
be aliased into the destination during the byte copy. A naive
in-place implementation would corrupt mid-copy; this test
pins that the result is the literal two-of-a concatenation.

    #test
    test_concat_self() -> unit
        val a = "xyz"
        val c = a + a
        assert(c == "xyzxyz", s"a + a should be 'xyzxyz', got '$c'")
        assert(len(c) == 6, "len 6")
        assert(a == "xyz", s"a should still be 'xyz', got '$a'")
